.bp 1
.sh 1 "INTRODUCTION"
.pp
The SMAL Hero cross assembler provides assembly language support for programming
the Motorola 6800 processor embedded in the Heathkit Hero-1 robot.
In addition to supporting the standard 6800 instruction mnemonics, the
SMAL Hero assembler supports a special set of mnemonics for use with the
Hero-1 robot control language interpreter.
The SMAL Hero assembler supports the assembly format and directives
of Machine Independent SMAL, a symbolic macro assembly language
developed at the University of
Iowa as a tool for teaching and research.
as such, it is a complete macro
assembly language and linkage editor system in a form which may be easily
transported to run on different host systems.
.pp
The object code produced by the SMAL Hero assembler is relocatable,
and it includes support for external symbols and FORTRAN common blocks.
Additionally, the assembler serves as a linker for SMAL Hero object
files; as such, it produces an absolute or relocatable
textual object code which may
be easily hand loaded, or loaded 
by a loader written in about 60 lines of Pascal code.
.pp
The basic SMAL assembler is coded so that it should produce correct results on
any machine which allows integers to range between -32768 and 32767.  This
has been verified by running the machine independent
version under versions of Pascal supporting 16 bit
two's complement arithmetic and 32 bit two's complement arithmetic.  It should
also work on any one's complement machine with at least 17 bit integer
representations.  The character set must include at least
55 printing characters and the machine independent version
has been tested
under a version of Pascal supporting a 64 character colating sequence and
under versions supporting ASCII with and without parity set in an 8 bit
byte.
The assembler does not use pointer variables but it does use recursion;
this may place limits on the languages into which it may easily be translated.
.pp
This document is an adaptation of:
.(l I
.ul 1
Machine Independent SMAL: A Symbolic Macro Assembly Language
  Douglas Jones, University of Iowa Computer Science Department
  Technical Report 82-06, May, 1982.
.)l
Additional information about SMAL is contained in:
.(l I
.ul 1
Assembly Language as Object Code
  Douglas Jones, Software - Practice and Experience, in press.
.)l
.bp
.sh 1 "NOTATION AND LEXICAL RULES"
.pp
The syntactic rules governing the formation of correct SMAL Hero programs
will be given in an extended form of BNF using the following special
symbols:
.(l I
< and > are used to enclose the names of syntactic elements.
          These elements will either have obvious meanings,
          or they will be defined somewhere in the text.
{ and } are used to enclose a group of syntactic elements
          which are optional and may be repeated an indefinite
          number of times.
[ and ] are used to enclose a group of syntactic elements
          which are optional and may not be repeated.
| is used to separate alternate groups of syntactic elements
          which may be used interchangably.
::= is used to define the named syntactic element on the left
          hand side as being replaceable by the group of
          syntactic elements on the right hand side.
.)l
The syntactic rules are supplemented, when needed, with text describing
the meaning of the constructs involved, and with illustrative examples.
.sh 2 "Lexical Structure"
.pp
SMAL programs may be described in two ways:  As sequences of lexical
elements with no structure, and in terms of syntactic structures.
At the lexical level, SMAL programs have the following structure:
.(l I
.ne 4
<program> ::= { { <space> } <lexeme> } { <space> } <line end> <end of file>
.)l
That is, a program is a file containing a sequence of lexemes;
lexemes may be separated by any number of blanks, which have no significance,
but lexemes may not contain blanks (except in quoted character strings).  The
legal lexemes are as follows:
.(l I
.ne 4
<lexeme> ::= <identifier>
           | <number>
           | <quoted string>
           | <line end>
           | :   -colon, used after labels and to take substrings.
           | .   -dot, used to refer to the location counter.
           | ,   -comma, used to separate arguments.
           | #   -pound sign, used for immediate operands.
           | =   -equals, used for assignment, comparison, and parameters.
           | >   -greater than, used for comparison.
           | <   -less than, used for comparison.
           | +   -plus, used for addition and as a unary sign.
           | -   -minus, used for subtraction and as a unary sign.
           | \\   -backslash, used for unary not.
           | ?   -question mark, a unary prefix on register numbers.
.ne 4
           | &   -ampersand, used for the and operator.
           | !   -exclamation point, used for the or operator.
           | (   -begin paren, used to begin a group or list.
           | )   -end paren, used to end a group or list.
.)l
The comments above describe the use of the single character lexemes.
The following additional punctuation marks are used within some lexemes:
.(l I
.ne 6
$  - used for hexadecimal and other radix numbers.
@  - used for octal and other radix numbers.
%  - used for binary and other radix numbers.
"   -used in quoted strings.
\&'   -used in quoted strings and in macro definitions.
;   -used to delimit comments at line ends.
.)l
The following ASCII punctuation marks are not used in the SMAL Hero
assembly language:
.(l I
[ ] { } `
.)l
The following ASCII punctuation marks are not used, but are likely to be used
in future revisions:
.(l I
.ne 3
*   -may be used for multiplication.
/   -may be used for division.
^   -may be used as a prefix on 'extended quotation marks'.
.ne 3
_   -may be allowed in identifiers.
|   -may be used as a synonym for !.
~   -may be used as a synonym for \\.
.)l
.sh 2 "Identifiers"
.pp
Identifiers begin with a letter followed by any number of letters or digits.
All characters in an identifier are significant.
.(l I
<identifier> ::= <letter> { <letter or digit> }
.)l
A consequence of this is that identifiers may not contain spaces and may not
cross from one line to the next.  Any implementation dependent line length
limit poses the only limit on the length of an identifier; typically, this
limit will be 80 characters.
Note that identifiers must be separated from any following decimal number
by at least one space to avoid ambiguity.
The following are legal identifiers:
.(l I
.ne 4
I
NEXT
THISISONLYONEIDENTIFIER
A1235B42XY
.)l
.ne 7
The following are not legal identifiers:
.(l I
76TROMBONES
THIS'IS_NOT.ONE-IDENTIFIER
.)l
The particular set of letters allowed may vary between implementations;
some will support only one case, and others will support both upper and
lower case.  If both cases are supported, case differences will be significant
in comparing identifiers.
.sh 2 "Numbers"
.pp
Numbers may take two basic forms:  They may be simple decimal numbers,
or they may be numbers in another radix (signified by a leading
radix specification).
Single character radix specifications are provided for hexadecimal (dollar
sign),
octal (at sign), and binary (percent sign); other bases may be used
by specifying the base as a decimal number, followed by one of these
special characters, followed by the number in that base.
.(l I
<number> ::= <decimal number>
           | <radix> <extended number> 
<radix> ::= <radix specification character>
          | <decimal number> <radix specification character>
<radix specification character> ::= $    -hexadecimal
                                  | @    -octal
                                  | %    -binary
<decimal number> ::= <digit> { <digit> }
<extended number> ::= <letter or digit> { <letter or digit> }
.)l
The radix, if explicitly specified, must be between 2 and 36 (inclusive), and
no digit must ever be greater than or equal to the radix.  The assembler
will raise the "bad radix" and "bad digit in number" errors when these
rules are violated.  Since the assembler uses a 16 bit internal
representation, no number with a magnitude greater than 65535 is allowed;
The assembler will produce a "value out of bounds" error if larger
numbers are used.  Note that numbers between 32768 and 65536 may be
interpreted as negative numbers in some situations, for example, when
performing comparisons.
Note that decimal numbers must be separated from any following hexadecimal,
octal, or binary
numbers by at least one space to distinguish the decimal number from a
radix specification.
The following legal numbers are all equal:
.(l I
.ne 3
32767
$7FFF
16%7FFF
@77777
.ne 3
8$77777
32@VVV
%111111111111111
.)l
.ne 6
The following are not legal numbers:
.(l I
65536
3276A
2$10120
40$00
.)l
.ne 5
The letters allowed as digits greater than 9 begin with A which has the value
10, and continue up to Z with the value 35.  The letter Z is only a valid digit
in base 36.  On systems allowing both upper and lower case letters, only
upper case letters should be used as extended digits.
.sh 2 "Quoted Strings"
.pp
Quoted strings may be formed with either single or double quotes, and may
be of any length that fits on one line.
.(l I
<quoted string> ::= " { <character> } "
                  | ' { <character> } '
.)l
Constraints on the length of a quoted string may be imposed by the
context in which it is used.  A "missing end quote" error will be raised
if the trailing quote is missing.
It should be noted that the single quote (apostrophe) has a special
meaning in macro definitions, and should be used with care.
The following are legal quoted strings:
.(l I
.ne 4
"GREAT'STRING'(("
"X"
\&''
\&')+"5"+('
.)l
.ne 7
The following are not legal strings:
.(l I
\&'DON''T DO IT'
"UNBALANCED'
.)l
Note especially that it is not possible to include a quote in a string by
repeating it, as in some high level languages.
.sh 2 "Comments and Line Ends"
.pp
All lines may end with a comment.  Comments begin with a semicolon
and may include any text.  Comments are completely ignored in the
normal assembly process, but they are stored as part of the
text of a macro, and macro parameter substitution does apply to them.
.(l I
<line end> ::= [ ; { <character> } ] <end of line>
.)l
Note that, unlike their use in high level languages descended from algol, it is
conventional to space the semicolon away from any immediately preceding
commands and to align the semicolons on successive lines to make a
column which delimits useful text from comments.
.bp
.sh 1 "BASIC FORMATS"
.pp
At the syntactic level,
a SMAL Hero program consists of a sequence of lines, each of which
may contain any number of labels, an assembly directive, and a comment.
Each of these fields is optional, and each may begin anywhere on the line,
as long as they appear in the required order.
Comments are defined at the lexical level and so are not formally treated
here.
.(l I
<program> ::= { <line> } <end of file>
<line> ::= { <label> } [ <directive> ] <line end>
.)l
Unlike many older assembly languages, these rules allow indenting and
blank lines to be used to clarify the structure of programs wherever this
is convenient.
.sh 2 "Labels"
.pp
A label is an indentifier followed by a colon.
The effect of using a
particular identifier as a label is that the value of that identifier
is set to the current assembly location and it becomes illegal to alter
that value in any way.
The initial value of the assembly location counter is relocatable zero, and
it is incremented once for each byte assembled.
.(l I
<label> ::= <identifier> :
.)l
Any attempt to redefine a label will result
in a "multiple label definition" error.  If the value of the location counter
differs between the two passes of the assembler, for example, as the result
of a macro expanding differently on the two passes, a "label differed in pass
1" error will result.
The following are legal lines of SMAL code containing labels:
.(l I
FIRST:
ANOTHER :    ;COMMENT
  AGAIN:
.)l
Note the lack of column restrictions and the use of optional spaces
between the identifier and the colon.
.sh 2 "Directives"
.pp
There are two kinds of assembler directives:  The first takes the form of
an assignment statement and is used to assign values to identifiers.
The second takes the form of a symbolic directive name
or instruction name followed by appropriate
operands.
.(l I
<directive> ::= <assignment> | <symbolic directive>
.)l
The forms of each of the symbolic directives will be documented in later
sections.
In general, each begins with an identifier which is either an op-code name
or the name of some assembly operation.  If an illegal identifier is used
in this context, an "invalid directive" error will result.
Directive names are not reserved words; that is, the name of a directive may
be used as a label without any conflict of meaning.
On systems allowing the use of both upper and lower case, directive
names must be in upper case.
.pp
The assignment directive assigns the value of an arbitrary expression to
an identifier.  An identifier assigned a value in this way may be redefined
at will, but it must not be used elsewhere as a label.
.(l I
<assignment> ::= <identifier> = <expression>
.)l
The following lines of legal SMAL Hero code demonstrate the use of assignment:
.(l I
.ne 3
NEG1 = -1
SIXTEENTHOUSAND=8000+8000
L:     THISLOC = L
.)l
Note that spaces are ignored before and after the equals sign, and that
labels may be used on the same line as an assignment.
The following lines demonstrate illegal assignments:
.(l I
.ne 6
MULDEF: MULDEF = 6
A = B
B = A
.)l
In the first case, the error is that an identifier used in a label cannot
be assigned to.  In the second and third cases, the definition is circular.
.pp
A special form of the assignment directive is used to modify the location
counter.  This is used, for example, to set the assembly origin.
.(l I
<assignment> ::= . = <expression>
.)l
Here, the special symbol "." (period) is used as if it were an identifier
standing for the location counter.
The location counter will take on both the magnitude and type of the
expression.  This means that an absolute assembly section may be begun
by assigning an absolute value such as a numeric constant to the location
counter, and a relocatable section may be begun by assigning an external
symbol, common name, or other relocatable value to the location counter.
By default, the location counter is initialized to relocatable zero.
The following lines of legal SMAL Hero code demonstrate the use of this form:
.(l I
.ne 5
BLOCK: .=.+5 ;BLOCK IS A LABEL ON 5 BYTES OF UNINITIALIZED MEMORY
A = .
\&. = COMMON
      ; CODE TO INITIALIZE LOCATIONS IN COMMON
\&. = A
.)l
In the first example above, the location counter is incremented by 5 bytes.
The remainder of the lines demonstrate how the current location can be
saved, changed to a different area, for example, a location in common, and
restored to where it was originally.
.sh 2 "Expressions"
.pp
Expressions are composed of a sequence of terms separated by binary
operators.  There is no operator precidence hierarchy, and terms are
evaluated and combined in a strict left to right order.  If a different
order is needed, parentheses may be used to construct a term which is
itself an expression.
.(l I
<expression> ::= <term> { <binary operator> <term> }
.)l
The allowed binary operators are:
.(l I
<binary operator> ::= + | -
                    | > | < | =
                    | & | ! | > > | < <
.)l
These stand for, respectively, the arithmetic operations of addition and
subtraction, the comparison operations greater than, less than, and equal to,
the boolean operations and and or, and the operatons left and right shift.
The following lines of legal SMAL Hero code demonstrate the use of expressions:
.(l I
.ne 4
FOUR     =  3 - 2 + 3
MINUSTWO = 3 - (2 + 3)
TRUE     = 5 = (3 + 2)
NONSENSE =   5 = 3+2
.)l
In the final example, the value of "NONSENSE" will not be the same as "TRUE"
because of left to right evaluation.  Note that spaces (or the lack of spaces)
around the operators have no effect on the order of evaluation.
.pp
The arithmetic operations are computed using 16 bit
binary numbers.  In the absence of other constraints, these may be treated
as either values in the range -32768 to 32767 or as values in the range
0 to 65535.
Overflow and underflow are not detected because it is impossible for the
assembler to determine which operands were intended to be signed or whether
or not the result should be interpreted as signed.
The following rules govern the type of the result when adding
or subtracting values of various types:
.(l I
   absolute + absolute    ==> absolute
   absolute + relocatable ==> relocatable
relocatable + absolute    ==> relocatable
relocatable + relocatable ==> error
   absolute - absolute    ==> absolute
   absolute - relocatable ==> error
relocatable - absolute    ==> relocatable
relocatable - relocatable ==> absolute or error
.)l
Note that, in the last case, the difference of two relocatable values
will only be defined if both are relocated relative to the same relocation
base.  A "misuse of relocation" error will result whenever an illegal
combination of operands is used.
.pp
The comparison operations are computed using 16 bit signed two's complement
numbers.  Thus, values will be interpreted as being in the range -32768 to
32767.  As a result, the constant 32768 will be reported to be less than
the constant 32767.  The results of the comparison operations will
be -1, 0 and 1, corresponding respectively to true, false and incomparable.
It is illegal to compare values of different types; thus, absolute
values may only be compared with other absolute values and not with
relocatable values.  Failure to follow this rule will result in a
"misuse of relocation" error and a value of 1.
.pp
The boolean and and or operators are computed by applying the basic
operation to corresponding pairs of bits from the operands to produce each
bit of the result.  Thus, these may be used for bit setting and testing
as well as for combining results from comparison operations in a boolean
expression.  These operations may only be applied to absolute values;
their application to relocatable values will result in a "misuse of relocation"
error.
.pp
The shift operators return the shifted value of their left operand, shifted
as many bits as is indicated by their right operand.  Unsigned shifts are used,
no sign extension or replication is performed on right shifts.  If the shift
count excedes 16, the result will be zero.  If the shift count is zero or
negative, the result will not be shifted.  Both operands must be absolute
values; a "misuse of relocation" error will result if relocatable values
are used.  Note that the operator symbols themselves consist of 2 lexemes;
thus, left shift may be written ">>" or ">\ >" with no change in meaning.
.sh 2 "Terms"
.pp
The terms of an expression consist of an optional unary operatior preceding
a value.  Thus, unary operators may be included before any operand used
in an expression.
.(l I
<term> ::= [ <unary operator> ] <value>
<unary operator> ::= + | - | \\ | ?
.)l
The allowed unary operators are a leading plus sign, a leading minus sign,
a leading not sign, and a leading question mark for register
specification.
The following examples demonstrate legal use of terms in SMAL Hero expressions:
.(l I
.ne 6
TRUE = ( +1 = 1 )
FALSE = \\ TRUE
THREE   = - 3 - - 6
NEGNINE = -(3 - - 6)
A = ?0
B = ?1
.)l
The middle two examples demonstrate that unary operators have a higher
precedence than binary operators.
The final two examples are the standard register definitions which should
be included at the head of each program for the 6800 so that the A and B
registers can be addressed symbolically.
.pp
The unary plus operator is always legal and has no effect on the associated
value.
.pp
The unary minus operator is used to negate the given value by taking the
two's complement.  It may only be applied to absolute values; it's use
with relocatable values will result in a "misuse of relocation" error.
.pp
The unary not operator is used to take the one's complement of the given value.
As such, it may be used for bit manipulation or to invert the sense of a
boolean value resulting, for example, from a comparison operation.
As with unary minus, unary not may only be applied to absolute values.
Note that unary not, when applied to the result of an illegal comparison,
returns a value which is neither true nor false.
.pp
The unary register operator is used to convert an integer to a register
number.  As such, it may only be applied to the absolute values 0 and 1.
Especially in macros, there are times when it is desirable to do arithmetic
on register names, such as in the following example:
.(l I
C = ?(?1-D)
.)l
Here, if D is a register name, perhaps passed to a macro as a parameter,
C will be set to the other register.
Thus, if D referred to the A register, C will refer to the B register.
.sh 2 "Values"
.pp
The values used to compose the terms of expressions may either be simple
values such as identifiers, numbers, strings, and references to the current
value of the
location counter, or they may be complex forms such as parenthesized
expressions and predefined functions.
.(l I
<value> ::= <identifier>
          | <number>
          | <quoted string>
          | .
          | ( <expression> )
          | <function>
.)l
When an identifier is used as a value, it must have a value assigned to
it somewhere in the program.  Simple forward references are allowed, and
are processed using two passes.  Thus, if an expression contains a forward
reference to some symbol, that symbol must have been defined by the end of
the first pass for that reference to be valid.
References to undefined identifiers will result in an "undefined symbol"
error.
When a dot is used as a value
in an expression, it is taken to stand for the current value of the
location counter.
An "unbalanced parentheses" error will be raised if the parentheses on an
expression do not balance.
.pp
When a quoted string is used as a value in a term of an expression,
the ASCII values of the characters will be used.  If the string contains
only one character, it will be stored in the least significant 8 bits
of the value.  If the string contains two characters, the first will be
stored in the most significant 8 bits, the second in the least significant
8 bits.  Unused bits of the value will be set to zero.  An attempt to use
a string longer than two characters as a value will result in a
"value out of bounds" error.
.sh 2 "Functions"
.pp
Special functions are provided to test whether or not a symbol is defined,
to test
whether it is a forward reference, to test the type of an expression,
and to test the length of a text fragment.
.(l I
<function> ::= DEF ( <identifier> )
             | FWD ( <identifier> )
             | TYP ( <expression> )
             | LEN ( <balanced string> )
.)l
Note that the form of the argument lists for these functions depends on the
particular function used.  An "invalid function" error will result if any
other identifier is used in a context where a function name is allowed.
Function names are not reserved words; the use of an identifier as a function
name is determined by the fact that it is followed by a begin parenthesis.
On systems allowing both upper and lower case letters, function names must
be in upper case.
.pp
The DEF function returns true (-1) if its argument has been defined previously
in the text of the program, either as a label or as the object of an
assignment.  Otherwise, if the argument is an identifier which has not
been previously defined, it returns false(0).  A "symbolic name expected"
error will be raised if the argument is not an identifier; in this case,
the value returned will be ambiguous (+1).
The following fragment of a SMAL Hero assembler listing demonstrates the use of
the DEF function:
.(l I
.ne 6
169                  ]DEF = 0
170 +0091: 00        ] B DEF(UNDEF)
171 +0092: FF        ] B DEF ( DEF )
172                  ]UNDEF = 0
.)l
Note that spaces have no effect, and that the DEF function responds
to whether or not its argument was previously defined in the text and thus
will return true only if it is defined earlier.
Thus, DEF will always have the same effect during both passes of the assembler.
.pp
The FWD function has the same form and syntactic requirements as the DEF
function, but it returns true when there is an unresolved forward reference
to the argument.  Thus, FWD will be false before the first reference to
the argument and false after the argument is defined, but true between
a reference to the argument and the later definition.
The following fragment of a SMAL Hero assembler listing demonstrates the use
of the FWD function.
.(l I
.ne 5
172 +0093: 00        ] B FWD(FWD)
173                  ]REF = FWD
174 +0094: FF        ] B FWD(FWD)
175                  ]FWD = 0
176 +0095: 00        ] B FWD(FWD)
.)l
Note that the use of an identifier as an argument to the DEF or FWD
function does not constitute a reference to that identifier from the point
of view of the FWD function.
.pp
The TYP function returns, as an absolute integer, the type of its argument.
As a general rule, the particular value returned by TYP will be meaningless,
except that it will be the same for all values relocated relative to the
same relocation base.  The only exception to this rule is that the TYP
applied to any absolute expression will return zero.
The following fragment of an assembler listing demonstrates the
use of the TYP function:
.(l I
.ne 4
176                  ]  EXT X
177 +0097: 00        ]L:B TYP(0)
178 +0098: FF        ]  B TYP(.) = TYP(L)
179 +0099: 00        ]  B TYP(X) = TYP(L)
.)l
Note the use of comparisons with the TYP function to see if the different
expressions are in fact comparable.
The TYP function operates by evaluating the expression given as an argument,
thus, use of an as yet undefined identifier as an argument to it constitutes
a forward reference to that identifier.
.pp
The LEN function returns, as an absolute integer, the length, in characters,
of its argument.  This is useful for testing, for example, whether a macro
parameter is present or absent.
The only constraint on the parameter to the LEN function is that it must
be lexically valid (contain no illegal numbers or strings), and if
it contains any parentheses, they must be properly balanced.
The length of the parameter will not be interpreted as including any
spaces before or after the parameter, but it will include any spaces
within the parameter.
The following fragment of an assembler listing demonstrates the use
of the LEN function:
.(l I
.ne 6
180 +009A: 00        ] B LEN()
181 +009B: 01        ] B LEN(0)
182 +009C: 02        ] B LEN('')
183 +009D: 04        ] B LEN( A  B )
184 +009E: 06        ] B LEN("A  B")
185 +009F: 07        ] B LEN(0 (0) 0)
.)l
Note that LEN, when applied to quoted strings, includes the quote marks
in its count.
.bp
.sh 1 "DATA STORAGE DIRECTIVES"
.pp
SMAL Hero supports directives to store bytes, words, and strings in memory.
The first two of these directives have the same syntax:
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= B <expression> { , <expression> }
                       | W <expression> { , <expression> }
.)l
The B and W directives store, respectively, one byte (8 bits) and one word
(16 bits) at the current location.
The location counter will be advanced by the number of bytes stored after
the expression is evaluated, so that, for example, "W\ ." will store a
pointer to itself.
If multiple operands are included, the additional operands are treated as if
they were processed by a new B or W directive on the next line.
If the value of the expression used on the B directive is greater than 255
or less than -128, a "value out of bounds" error will be raised.  Note that
"B\ 128" and "B\ -128" will store exactly the same value; in the first
case, it was probably intended to be used as an unsigned 8 bit value, in
the second case, it was probably intended to be used as a signed 8 bit value.
The following sample assembly listing demonstrates the use of the B and W
directives for various purposes:
.(l I
.ne 10
23 +000F: FF        ]L: B 255
24 +0010: 00FF      ]   W 255
25 +0012: FFFF      ]   W 65535
26 +0014: FF        ]   B 8$377
27 +0015: F0        ]   B 2$11110000
28 +0016: 7FFF      ]   W 32$VVV
29 +0018: 20        ]   B " "
30 +0019:+000F      ]   W L
31 +001B:+0010      ]   W L+1
32 +001D:+001D      ]   W .
.)l
In the above example, the location counter was relocatable, as indicated by
the leading plus sign in the location column in the listing.  The values on
the last 3 lines of the listing are relocatable because they are derived from
the location counter.
.pp
Strings of ASCII characters may be stored using the ASCII directive.  This
takes a quoted string as an operand.
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= ASCII <quoted string> { , <expression> }
.)l
Note that the 7 bit ASCII representation of each character in the string
is stored right justified in one byte, and that the most significant bit
of that byte is set to zero.
On the 6800, "ASCII\ 'ab'" and "W\ 'ab'" will store the
same result in memory because the 6800 stores the most significant
byte of a word first in memory, like the IBM 360, unlike the PDP 11.
The following fragment of a SMAL Hero assembly listing demonstrates the correct
use of the ASCII directive:
.(l I
.ne 7
63 +003F: 41        ]   ASCII "A"
64 +0040: 41  42    ]   ASCII "AB"
65                  ]CR = $0D
66                  ]LF = $0A
67 +0042: 4E  4F    ]MSG: ASCII "NOW IS THE TIME FOR ALL GOOD MEN"
68 +0062: 0D        ]     B CR
69 +0063: 0A        ]     B LF
.)l
In this example, note that the control characters CR and LF have been
appended to the string by using additional B directives.
These could have been appended to the string by including them as
additional operands on the ASCII directive itself, as follows:
.(l I
67 +0042: 4E  4F    ]MSG: ASCII "NOW IS THE TIME FOR ALL GOOD MEN",CR,LF
.)l
This has exactly the same meaning as the original.  In general,
if the additional operands on an ASCII directive are quoted strings,
they will be treated exactly as if they appeared in a new ASCII directive
on the next line; otherwise, they will be treated as if they appeared as
operands on a B directive.
.bp
.sh 1 "EXTERNAL SYMBOLS"
.pp
SMAL Hero allows the results of assembling a number of source programs to be
combined into a single program at run-time by a linkage editor.
Because of this, there
are two classes of symbols in SMAL, local symbols and global symbols.
Local symbols are defined only within one particular source program, and the
same name may be used with a different meaning within another.  Global
symbols, on the other hand, are shared by all programs which are to be linked
into one final program.  There are two kinds of global symbols:  Those which
are defined internally in one program and referenced in another, and those
which are not defined in any particular file but are expected to be defined
by the linker.
.sh 2 "External Declarations"
.pp
When a symbol defined in one program is to be referenced by another program,
it must be declared as being external to the program which uses it.
This is done by defining that identifier with an external directive:
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= EXT <identifier> { , <identifier> }
.)l
Identifiers which are declared as external symbols may not be used as
labels and they may not have values assigned to them; violation of this
rule will result in a "multiple label definition" error.
Identifiers used in this context must not have been previously referenced
in the text of the program; a
"name used before defined" error will result when this rule is violated.
Multiple identifiers may be declared with one EXT directive.
.pp
In addition to relocatable symbols introduced by defining labels in terms
of relocatable location counter values, each external symbol introduces a new
relocation base relative to which additional symbols may be defined.
Thus, it is legal to add or subtract absolute values to or from an external
symbol name in order to reference adjacent values (if the name is externally
defined as a label, the adjacent values will reference adjacent memory
locations).
The following fragment of a SMAL Hero assembler listing illustrates the use of
external declarations:
.(l I
.ne 4
140                  ]   EXT EXT
141                  ]ADR = EXT + 8
142 +007A:+0000      ]   W EXT
143 +007C:+0008      ]   W ADR
.)l
Note that the external symbol "EXT" in the above example is used as a
relocation base for the symbol "ADR".
.sh 2 "Internal Declarations"
.pp
When a program wishes to allow other programs to reference a locally defined
symbol, it must be declared to be accessible by an internal directive:
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= INT <identifier> { , <identifier> }
.)l
Identifiers declared as internal must be defined somewhere in the program;
failure to follow this rule will result in an "undefined symbol" error.
Within the program where an internal symbol is defined, that symbol may
be manipulated as if it were an entirely local symbol.  The value of that
symbol at the end of the second assembly pass will be the value which is
exported.
Multiple identifiers may be declared by one INT directive.
The following fragment of legal SMAL Hero code illustrates the use of
internal directives:
.(l I
.ne 4
      INT EXT
      INT CONST, CONST1
EXT:  CONST = 5
      CONST1 = 6
.)l
Note that the internal symbol "EXT" is declared as a label, so it probably
has a relocatable value, but the internal symbol "CONST" is declared by
assigning an absolute integer value to it.
.sh 2 "Common Declarations"
.pp
When a program wishes to reference an area of memory which is to be allocated
by the linkage editor, that area must be declared to be a common block
and its size must be specified:
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= COMMON <identifier> , <expression>
.)l
Common names may be used exactly like any external symbol, and must satisfy
the same rules.  Thus, the name must not have been referenced previous to the
common directive, and it must not be defined anywhere else in the program.
.pp
The second argument to the common directive gives the size of the region
which the linkage editor is expected to allocate.  This must be either an
absolute value, in bytes, or it must be relocated relative to the common
base; any other form will result in a "misuse of relocation" error.
Generally, the declarations of the same common in the different programs
linked by the linker should be the same.  If different sizes are
specified, the first size encountered by the linker will be used and later
sizes will be ignored.
.pp
The following fragment of an assembler listing illustrates the correct
use of the COMMON directive:
.(l I
.ne 8
227                  ]COMMON COM1,6
228                  ]COMMON COM2,COM2LIM
229                  ]LOCSAVE = .
230                  ]. = COM2
231 +0000: 05        ]B 5
232 +0001: 06        ]B 6
233                  ]COM2LIM:
234                  ]. = LOCSAVE
.)l
Note that the size of "COM1" above is declared with a simple absolute value,
but that the size of "COM2" is declared by using a label relocated relative
to "COM2" itself.  The pattern of code used to initialize "COM2" is suggested
as a standard for assembly time initialization of commons.
.bp
.sh 1 "CONDITIONAL ASSEMBLY"
.pp
SMAL Hero supports structured conditional assembly facilities which provide
an assembly-time control structure comparable to the corresponding run-time
control structures of many high level languages.
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= IF <expression>
                       | ELSEIF <expression>
                       | ELSE
                       | ENDIF
.)l
The semantics of the conditional directives is best described in terms
of conditional blocks.  A conditional block begins with an if block
and ends with an endif directive.  Between the if block and the endif
directive, an optional sequence of elseif blocks is allowed, followed by
an optional else block.  Each block is headed by a specific directive
which controls the assembly of the following sequence of lines, including
possible nested conditional blocks.
.(l I
<conditional block> ::= <if block>
                      { <elseif block> }
                      [ <else block> ]
                        ENDIF
<if block> ::= IF <expression> <line end> { <line> }
<elseif block> ::= ELSEIF <expression> <line end> { <line> }
<else block> ::= ELSE <line end> { <line> }
.)l
The lines within an if or an elseif block are only assembled if the
corresponding boolean expression evaluated to true (-1).
The lines within an elseif or else block will only be assembled if no previous
if or elseif block within that conditional block was assembled.
No more than one sub-block within a conditional
block will ever be assembled.
A "missing endif" error will be raised if an if block is not correctly
terminated before the end of a macro, text insertion, or program.
.pp
It is inappropriate to use labels on the if, elseif, else, and endif
directives, but they are allowed.  If they are used, they will be considered
to be part of the immediately preceding sequence of lines.  Thus, the label
on an elseif directive will be processed as if it were part of the previous
if or elseif block.
.sh 2 "Examples"
.pp
The following fragments of a SMAL Hero assembler listing illustrate valid uses
of the conditional assembly directives:
.(l I
.ne 6
199                  ]IF VERSION < 5
200 +00AD: 00        ]  LOWV: B 0
201                  ]ENDIF
.)l
The above example might be used to declare and initialize a variable
which is only used in some versions of a program.
.(l I
.ne 8
208                  ]IF VERSION < 2
209                  ]  STR: ASCII "SIMPLE VERSION"
210                  ]ELSEIF VERSION < 4 ;AND VERSION > 1
211 +00AF: 4D  45    ]  STR: ASCII "MEDIUM VERSION"
212                  ]ELSE ;VERSION > 3
213                  ]  STR: ASCII "COMPLEX VERSION"
214                  ]ENDIF
215 +00BD: 00        ]B 0
.)l
The above example might be used to create the part of the title string
of a program which depends on the version.
The comments used on the ELSEIF and
ELSE directives document assertions which are known
to be true on entry to the corresponding block and therefore need not be
explicitly tested for.
The following example is equivalent to the above one, but it uses a nested
conditional instead of an elseif directive:
.(l I
.ne 13
208                  ]STR: IF VERSION < 2
209                  ]         ASCII "SIMPLE"
210                  ]     ELSE
211                  ]         IF VERSION < 4
212 +00AF: 4D  45    ]             ASCII "MEDIUM"
213                  ]         ELSE
214                  ]             ASCII "COMPLEX"
215                  ]         ENDIF
216                  ]     ENDIF
217 +00B5: 20  56    ]     ASCII " VERSION"
218 +00BD: 00        ]     B 0
.)l
.bp
.sh 1 "MACRO ASSEMBLY"
.pp
The SMAL Hero macro facilities allow assembly-time control structures comparable
to the procedure structures of many high level languages.
A macro is a named block of text which is assembled into the program wherever
its name occurs in the context of a macro call.
If a macro is defined as having formal parameters, the corresponding actual
parameters are substituted for the formal parameters within the body of the
macro before it is assembled.
.sh 2 "Macro Definitions"
.pp
Each macro definition begins with a macro directive and ends with an endmac
directive.  The macro directive is followed by the name of the macro and its
formal parameter list.
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= MACRO <identifier> [ <formal list> ]
                       | ENDMAC
.)l
The semantics of a macro declaration are most easily described in terms of
macro blocks which begin with a macro directive and end with an endmac
directive.  The body of the block between the two is saved by the assembler
and assembled as a result of each call to the macro.
.(l I
<macro block> ::= MACRO <identifier> [ <formal list> ] <line end>
                { <line> }
                  ENDMAC
.)l
Note that any labels on the line containing the macro directive are
not interpreted as having anything to do with the macro but are processed
as normal labels.  Labels on the line containing the endmac directive are
completely ignored!
If a macro block is not properly terminated with an endmac directive,
a "missing endmac" error will result.
The following fragment of valid SMAL Hero code illustrates
the definition of a simple
parameterless macro:
.(l I
.ne 7
MACRO CRLF
  B $0D
  B $0A
ENDMAC
.)l
A call to this macro can be used after an ASCII directive to put a CR and LF
after the text string.
.pp
Macro definitions may contain other macro definitions, but the effect is not
that of creating a local macro, as might be expected by anology with
high level languages.  Instead, the internal macro definition will be
processed by the assembler each time the containing macro is called.
Thus, a macro may be used to create a number of other macros by calling
it repeatedly with different parameters.
.sh 2 "Macro Formal Parameters"
.pp
The formal parameter list on a macro definition lists the symbolic names
of each parameter and the parameter passing mode.  Symbolic parameter
names are identifiers.
There is an implementation dependent limit on the number of formal parameters
allowed; if this is exceded, a "too many macro parameters" error will be
raised.  Typically, at least 8 parameters will be allowed.
.(l I
<formal list> ::= <formal parameter> { , <formal parameter> }
<formal parameter> ::= <identifier>
                     | ( <identifier> )
                     | = <identifier>
.)l
The three macro parameter modes are:  Name mode, in which the formal parameter
is declared by just its name; list mode, in which the formal parameter name
is surrounded by parentheses; and value mode, in which the formal parameter
name is preceded by an equals sign.  The treatment of actual parameters in
each of these three modes will be discussed later.
The following fragment of a SMAL assembly listing illustrates
the definition of a simple macro
with one parameter passed by name:
.(l I
.ne 6
MACRO ASCIICRLF TEXT
   ASCII TEXT
   CRLF
ENDMAC
.)l
Note that the above macro contains a call to the macro "CRLF" defined in the
previous example.
.pp
Within the macro body, each occurance of any macro formal parameter name
is identified as the macro is defined.  Formal parameters will be identified
within comments and within character strings.  The only time that a formal
parameter will not be identified is when it is embedded in a larger
identifier.  For example, if "A" and "B" are formal parameters, neither
of them will be identified in the string "AB".  If "AB" is to be seen
as the concatenation of two parameters, an apostrophe should be inserted
between the two, as in "A'B".  Within the body of any macro, single
apostrophes will be deleted, and strings of two or more apostrophes will
be shortened by one when that macro is defined.  Thus, special care must
be taken when using apostrophes as single quotes around quoted strings.
.pp
.sh 2 "Macro Calls"
.pp
A macro call takes the form of a symbolic directive which begins with
the name of a previously defined macro, followed by a list of actual
parameters.
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= <identifier> [ <actual list> ]
<actual list> ::= <actual parameter> { , <actual parameter> }
<actual parameter> ::= [ <by name> | <by list> | <by value> ]
.)l
The first step in processing a macro call is to store the text of each
actual parameter, according to the parameter passing rules associated
with that parameter type.
Individual parameters may be omitted or blank, in which case, no text is
passed.  In all cases, the number of actual parameters must be less than
or equal to the number of formal parameters.
After the parameters are stored, assembly of the body of
the macro begins.  As the body is being assembled, all formal parameters
which had been identified at the time the body was originally
defined are replaced with the text of the corresponding actual parameters.
If the resulting text for any line is longer than the implementation defined
line length limit, the error "text too long for line" will result.
.pp
When a formal parameter is declared as a name parameter, the actual parameter
may be any text which does not include commas unless the commas are
parenthesized and as long as any parentheses in the text are balanced.
The entire text of a name parameter will be passed with no changes other than
to remove leading and trailing blanks.
.pp
When a formal parameter is declared as a list parameter, the actual parameter
must be enclosed in parentheses and may contain any lexically valid text as
long as the
parentheses it contains are balanced.  A substring specification is allowed
after the end parenthesis; this consists of two expressions set off from
the parameter and each other by colons.
.(l I
<by list> ::= ( <balanced string> ) [ <substring> ]
<substring> ::= : [ <expression> ] [ : <expression> ]
.)l
If the substring specification is absent, the entire balanced string
will be passed as a parameter.
Note that the enclosing parentheses are never passed.
If the first expression in the substring
specification is present, it specifies the index of the first character in
the balanced string to be passed (the default value of the first expression
is one).  If the second expression in the substring specification is
present, it specifies how many characters from the balanced string are
to be passed (the default value of the second expression is "all").
Substring specifications should be used with care because they may introduce
strings with unbalanced parentheses.
.pp
When a formal parameter is declared as a value parameter, the actual parameter
must be an expression.
.(l I
<by value> ::= <expression>
.)l
The expression will be evaluated at the time of the macro call;
if the result is an absolute value, the text of the decimal representation
of that value will be passed as a parameter.  Note that the decimal
representation used will be unsigned, that is, -1 will be passed as 65535;
this allows identifiers to be constructed by appending the value of the
parameter to a letter.
If the value is not absolute, an expression will be passed which evaluates
to the desired value.
.pp
In no case will a macro call be executed if any errors are found in the
actual parameter list.
During the assembly of the body of a macro, other macro calls may be
processed, even recursive calls.
.sh 2 "Examples"
.pp
The following fragments of SMAL Hero assembly listings demonstrate the correct
use of macros.
The first example is contrived to demonstrate the use of each parameter
passing mode:
.(l I
.ne 4
249                  ]MACRO T NAME,=NUMBER,(LIST)
250                  ]   NAME NUMBER
251                  ]   LIST
252                  ]ENDMAC
253                  ]
.ne 4
254                  ]T B,5+4,(COMMON A,5)
254 +00BA: 09        ]   B 9
254                  ]   COMMON A,5
254                  ]END
255                  ]
.ne 4
256                  ]T W,-1,(BIB -25):3
256 +00BB: FFFF      ]   W 65535
256 +00BD: E7        ]   B -25
256                  ]END
257                  ]
.ne 4
258                  ]T W,A+2,("; NONESUCH"):2:6
258 +00BE:+0002      ]   W A+2
258                  ]   ; NONE
258                  ]END
.)l
In the first call to the macro, note that the expression "5+4" has been
evaluated, and the value "9" has been substituted into the macro body.
Similarly, in the second call, the expression "-1" was evaluated and
passed as "65536".  In the third call, the expression "A+2" could not
be passed as a number because it is relocated relative to the common name
"A", which was defined in the first call to the macro.
.pp
The following two macros demonstrate the use of value parameters and
concatenation for automatic label generation.
The first macro simply concatenates its three parameters, with the constraint
that the middle parameter is an expression.  This is used to construct
new symbols by the second macro.  The second macro maintains a counter, "LAB",
which is incremented once each time it is called.  Thus, each time it is
called, it will be able to create new symbols.
.(l I
.ne 3
 2                  ]MACRO LABGEN (PART1),=NUM,(PART2)
 3                  ]   PART1'NUM'PART2
 4                  ]ENDMAC
 5                  ]
.ne 7
 6                  ]LAB = 1
 7                  ]MACRO LABUSER
 8                  ]   LAB = LAB + 1
 9                  ]   LABGEN (L),LAB,(:)
10                  ]   B 0
11                  ]   LABGEN (W L),LAB
12                  ]ENDMAC
13                  ]
.ne 10
14                  ]LABUSER
14                  ]   LAB = LAB + 1
14                  ]   LABGEN (L),LAB,(:)
14                  ]   L2:
14                  ]END
14 +0000: 00        ]   B 0
14                  ]   LABGEN (W L),LAB
14 +0001:+0000      ]   W L2
14                  ]END
14                  ]END
15                  ]
.ne 10
16                  ]LABUSER
16                  ]   LAB = LAB + 1
16                  ]   LABGEN (L),LAB,(:)
16                  ]   L3:
16                  ]END
16 +0003: 00        ]   B 0
16                  ]   LABGEN (W L),LAB
16 +0004:+0003      ]   W L3
16                  ]END
16                  ]END
.)l
Note that the symbol "LAB" could have been initialized in the macro "LABUSER"
instead of in the main program.  To do this, the initialization would have
to be assembled conditionally depending on whether or not "LAB" had been
previously defined.
.pp
The last example illustrates a macro which is almost equivalent
to the ASCII directive.  This macro redefines the ASCII directive
and is compatable with it for most strings.
.(l I
.ne 10
 1                  ]MACRO ASCII STRING,(PART1),(PART2)
 2                  ]  IF LEN(STRING) > 0
 3                  ]    ASCII ,(STRING):2:1,(STRING):3:LEN(STRING)-3
 4                  ]  ELSEIF LEN(''PART1'') > 2
 5                  ]    LIST + 100
 6                  ]    B ''PART1''
 7                  ]    LIST - 100
 8                  ]    ASCII ,(PART2):1:1,(PART2):2
 9                  ]  ENDIF
10                  ]ENDMAC
.ne 7
11 +0000: 00        ]B 0
12                  ]ASCII "CALC"
12 +0001: 43        ]    B 'C'
12 +0002: 41        ]    B 'A'
12 +0003: 4C        ]    B 'L'
12 +0004: 43        ]    B 'C'
13 +0005: 01        ]B 1
.)l
This example demonstrates the combination of conditional and macro assembly,
the use of substring parameters, the use of the function LEN to detect missing
parameters, and the use of recursion.  Note also the use of the listing
control directive and the use of doubled single quotes within the macro
definition which result in single quotes at expansion time.
.bp
.sh 1 "MISCELANEOUS ASSEMBLY DIRECTIVES"
.pp
.sh 2 "Text Instertions"
.pp
SMAL Hero allows text to be inserted into the body of a program from
an alternate source file at assembly time.  The only requirements are that
the alternate source file be itself a legal SMAL program, and that insertions
are not nested deeper than the implementation allows.  The USE directive
causes text insertion.
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= USE <quoted string>
.)l
The string operand on a USE directive must contain the name of a file
from which additional text may be read.
The USE directive is not executed by a preprocessor; thus, the inclusion of
USE directives in the body of a macro will have no effect until that
macro is called, and the inclusion of USE directives in the body of
a conditional block will only have an effect if the expressions governing
the execution of that conditional block have appropriate values.
If too many levels of nested USE directives are encountered, a "too many use
levels" error will be raised.
Typically, at least 2 levels of nesting will be allowed, so that one
used file can use another.
.pp
USE directives are commonly used for inserting standard macro definitions
into a program, or for inserting common definitions into one of a group
of programs which are to be linked together.
The following two source files demonstrate the use of the USE directive:
.(l I
.ne 4
; FILE OF STANDARD DEFINITIONS
A = ?0
B = ?1

.ne 5
; A USER FILE
USE "STANDARD"
LDA B #5
LDA A #2
.)l
If the first file is stored in a file known to the operating
system as "STANDARD", assembling the second file will result in the
following listing:
.(l I
.ne 9
 1                  ]; A USER FILE
 2                  ]USE "STANDARD"
 3 +0000: C6  05    ]LDA B #5
 4 +0004: 96  02    ]LDA A #2
.)l
Note that, by default, the contents of the used file are not listed
as they are processed.
For example, in the above listing, the contents of "STANDARD" are not listed,
but the assembler has seen its contents, as is shown by the
values produced when "A" and "B" were used.
.pp
When a hierarchically structured file system is used where there is a
clear syntactic distinction between absolute file names and relative
file names, the SMAL Hero assembler will interpret relative file names
in the context of the directory immediately containing the current
source file.  For example, under Unix, if the assembler is currently
reading from "library/dir" and the directive "USE\ 'entry'" is
encountered, the assembler will read from "library/entry".  In the
same context, if the directive "USE\ '/usr/public/lib'" will result
in reading from "/usr/public/lib", since that file name was specified
absolutely.
.sh 2 "End of File"
.pp
The assembler automatically inserts an END directive at the end of each
source file from which it reads, and at the end of each macro body it
processes.  The only function of the END directive is to mark the ends of files
and macros.
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= END
.)l
It is legal to explicitly include an END directive at the end of a file; if
additional lines are included in the file after the end directive, they will
always be ignored.
.pp
Explicit inclusion of an END directive in a macro body requires that the
END directive be enclosed in single quotes to prevent it from being mistaken
for the end of file.
About the only reason to do this is to include special comments on the end
of a macro or to adjust the indenting of the END directive.
.sh 2 "Starting Address Specification"
.pp
The assembler allows the address at which the object program is to begin
execution to be passed through to the loader by the use of the start directive.
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= S <expression>
.)l
Only one S directive may be given per program, and it is suggested that
that directive be given at the head of the program so that it is easy
to find.
A typical program might begin as follows:
.(l I
        TITLE   MAIN PROGRAM
        S       START
START:  LDS     #STACK
.)l
If no starting address is specified, a default may be
established by the loader.
.sh 2 "Listing Control"
.pp
The assembler normally only lists source lines from the base file from
which it is assembling, and does not list the results of macro calls and
text insertions.  The listing behavior may be manually controlled by the
list directive.
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= LIST <expression>
.)l
At all times, the assembler keeps a record of the number of macro nesting
levels it is to include in the listing.  The operand of the LIST directive
is added to this counter.  Thus, if the operand is positive, it will cause
additional nesting levels to be listed, and if it is negative, it will
suppress the listing of some number of nesting levels.
.pp
The line containing the LIST directive will not be printed if it causes
listing to be turned on or off.  Thus, macros which use LIST directives
to selectively list parts of their expansions will not clutter the listing
with the LIST directives themselves.
.pp
By default, SMAL Hero listings are titled with the version of the assembler
and the name of the file being assembled.
The following two directives may be used to modify this behavior:
.(l I
.ne 2
<symbolic directive> ::= TITLE { <lexeme> }
                       | SUBTITLE { <lexeme> }
.)l
The subtitle on each listing page will appear directly below the title.
The remainder of the line following the TITLE or SUBTITLE directive is
used as the title or subtitle, including comments.  The text of the title
or subtitle must be lexically valid; this usually causes no problems, since
any sequence of words is lexically valid as a sequence of identifiers.
.pp
Programs should usually contain only one TITLE directive, and may contain
any number of SUBTITLE directives.  Each SUBTITLE directive will force the
start of a new page in the listing, and in addition, will be used during
the first pass of the assembler to construct a table of contents.
Additional page feeds may be included in the listing by the use of the
PAGE directive:
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= PAGE
.)l
PAGE directives and SUBTITLE directives encountered while listing is
disabled will cause a page eject, but the directive itself will not
be listed.  Some effort has been made to suppress multiple page ejects;
specifically, a PAGE directive followed by a SUBTITLE directive will cause
only one eject.
.bp
.sh 1 "M6800 INSTRUCTIONS"
.pp
The SMAL Hero assembler accepts the standard M6800 instruction mnemonics
used by the Motorola MPU Cross Assembler, MPCASM.
Complete documentation of the meanings of the various instructions can
be found in the
'ul 1
M6800 Microprocessor Programming Manual
and other sources.
The M6800 supports nine basic instruction formats:
.(l I
<symbolic directive> ::= <memory to register>
                       | <register to memory>
                       | <memory to implicit>
                       | <implicit to memory>
                       | <unary memory or register>
                       | <implicit>
                       | <stack>
                       | <relative>
                       | <jump>
.)l
The assembly formats for each of these instruction classes are
documented below, along with a list of the applicable instructions:
.(l I
<memory to register> ::= <m-r opcode> [ , ] <register> [ , ] <m-r address>
<m-r opcode> ::= ADD | ADC | AND | BIT | CMP
               | EOR | LDA | ORA | SUB | SBC
<register> ::= <expression>
<m-r address> ::= # <expression>    -- immediate
                | <expression>      -- direct or extended
                | <expression> , X  -- indexed
.)l
Note that the distinction between the direct and extended addressing
modes is made on the basis of the value of the expression;
absolute values between 0 and 255 are assembled as direct addresses,
forward references and relocatable values are assembled as extended
addresses.
.(l I
.ne 6
<register to memory> ::= <r-m opcode> [ , ] <register> [ , ] <r-m address>
<r-m opcode> ::= STA
<r-m address> ::= <expression>      -- direct or extended
                | <expression> , X  -- indexed
.)l
Note that immediate mode is not allowed on the STA instruction.
.(l I
.ne 5
<memory to implicit> ::= <m-i opcode> [ , ] <m-r address>
<m-i opcode> ::= CPX | LDX | LDS
.)l
Note that for the memory to implicit instructions, immediate operands are
16 bits long instead of the usual 8.
.(l I
.ne 4
<implicit to memory> ::= <i-m opcode> [ , ] <r-m address>
<i-m opcode> ::= STX | STS
.)l
Note that the implicit to memory opcodes do not support immediate mode.
.(l I
.ne 4
<unary memory or register> ::= <u opcode> [ , ] <u address>
<u opcode> ::= CLR | COM | NEG | DEC | INC
             | ROL | ROR | ASL | ASR | LSR
             | TST
.ne 7
<u address> ::= <register>
              | <expression>       -- extended
              | <expression> , X   -- indexed
.)l
Note that for the unary instructions, the distinction between registers
and extended addresses is made on the basis of the type of the value of
the expression.
.(l I
.ne 8
<implicit> ::= <i opcode>
<i opcode> ::= ABA | CBA | DAA | SBA | TAB
               TBA | DEX | DES | INX | INS
               TXS | TSX | NOP | RTI | RTS
               SWI | WAI | CLC | CLI | CLV
               SEC | SEI | SEV | TAP | TPA
.)l
These instructions have no operands specified in the assembly language.
.(l I
.ne 5
<stack> ::= <s opcode> [ , ] <register>
<s opcode> ::= PSH | PUL
.)l
Note on the stack operations that the only legal operands are expressions
specifying a register.
.(l I
.ne 9
<relative> ::= <r opcode> [ , ] <expression>
<r opcode> ::= BRA | BCC | BCS | BEQ | BGE
             | BGT | BHI | BLE | BLS | BLT
             | BMI | BNE | BVC | BVS | BPL
             | BSR
.)l
Note on the relative operations that the operand must be an expression
relocated relative to the current relocation base, specifying an address
within the relative branching range from the current location.
.(l I
.ne 5
<jump> ::= <j opcode> [ , ] <j address>
<j opcode> ::= JMP | JSR
<j address> ::= <expression>       -- extended
              | <expression> , X   -- indexed
.)l
For the jump instructions, the expression must evaluate to a valid memory
address.
.bp
.sh 1 "MPCASM COMPATABILITY MACROS"
.pp
The Motorola MPU cross assembler MPCASM supports a number of assembly
directives which correspond to directives supported by the SMAL Hero
assembler, but have different names.  A set of standard macro definitions
can be used to define the MPCASM mnemonics for these directives.
.pp
The MPCASM data storage directives FCB, FDB, and FCC correspond directly
to the SMAL Hero B, W, and ASCII directives; the macros used to perform
the translation are given below:
.(l I
.ne 3
MACRO FCB =EXPR
 B EXPR
ENDMAC

.ne 3
MACRO FDB =EXPR
 W EXPR
ENDMAC

.ne 3
MACRO FCC STRING
 ASCII STRING
ENDMAC
.)l
The MPCASM ORG and RMB directives are used to manipulate the location
counter and may be simulated in SMAL Hero assembly language
by explicit assignment to the location
counter, as in the following macros:
.(l I
.ne 3
MACRO ORG =EXPR
 . = EXPR
ENDMAC

.ne 3
MACRO RMB =EXPR
 . = . + EXPR
ENDMAC
.)l
The MPCASM PAGE directive is equivalent to the SMAL Hero PAGE directive,
so no translation is needed.
The MPCASM NAM directive is used to name an assembly listing, so it can be
mapped to the SMAL Hero TITLE directive, as follows:
.(l I
.ne 3
MACRO NAM NAME
 TITLE NAME
ENDMAC
.)l
The MPCASM SPC directive can be safely replaced by a sequence of blank lines
in the SMAL Hero assembly language, but the following macro approximates
its function by including the requested number of spaces in the listing.
Note that, unlike MPCASM, the SMAL Hero assembler will list the SPC directive
itself.
.(l I
.ne 9
MACRO SPC =EXPR
 IF EXPR > 0
  LIST + 100

  LIST - 100
  SPC EXPR - 1
 ENDIF
ENDMAC
.)l
The MPCASM END directive will accomplish the same function with the SMAL Hero
assembler, but it will also terminate the assembly process, causing any
following text to be ignored (including any following MON directive).
There is no way to define a macro which accomplishes the same function as
the MPCASM EQU and OPT directives, although the simple textual substitution
of an equals sign will solve this problem for EQU.
.bp
.sh 1 "HERO-1 ROBOT CONTROL LANGUAGE INSTRUCTIONS"
.pp
In addition to supporting the M6800 microprocessor, the SMAL Hero assemblrer
supports the Heathkit Hero-1 Robot Control Language interpreter which is
supported by the standard Heathkit ROM in the Hero-1 robot.
Further information on the Robot Control Language can be found in the
'ul 1
HERO ROBOT Model ET-18 Technical Manual
and in the
'ul 1
Heathkit User's Manual for the HERO ROBOT Model ET-18.
The Robot control language instructions have the following formats:
.(l I
.ne 8
<symbolic directive> ::= <rcl implicit>
                       | <rcl branch>
                       | <rcl indexed>
                       | <rcl time>
                       | <rcl jump>
                       | <rcl motormove>
                       | <rcl moveall>
.)l
The assembly formats of each of these instruction classes are documented
below, along with the names of the applicable instructions.
.(l I
.ne 5
<rcl implicit> ::= ZERO   -- zero
                 | RTE    -- return to executive
                 | CRL    -- change to robot language
                 | CML    -- change to machine language
.ne 5
                 | ADM    -- abort drive motor
                 | ASM    -- abort steering motor
                 | AAM    -- abort arm motors
                 | ASP    -- abort speech
.ne 6
                 | ELD    -- enable light detector
                 | ESD    -- enable sound detector
                 | EUR    -- enable ultrasonic ranging
                 | EMD    -- enable motion detector
                 | ED     -- enable display
.ne 6
                 | DLD    -- disable light detector
                 | DSD    -- disable sound detector
                 | DMD    -- disable motion detector
                 | DUR    -- disable ultrasonic ranging
                 | DD     -- disable display
.)l
Note that the CRL mnemonic is a synonym for the 6800 SWI instruction,
which raises a software interrupt which is interpreted by the Heathkit
monitor as requesting a change to Robot Control Language.
.(l I
.ne 6
<rcl branch> ::= <rcl b opcode> [ , ] <expression>
<rcl b opcode> ::= BBB    -- branch if base busy
                 | BSB    -- branch if steering busy
                 | BAB    -- branch if arm busy
                 | BSP    -- branch if speaking
.)l
As with the 6800 branch instructions, the operand expression on the
Robot Control Language branch instructions must evaluate to an address
which can be reached by an 8 bit offset from the location of the
instruction.
.(l I
.ne 6
<rcl indexed> ::= <rcl i opcode> [ , ] <j address>
<rcl i opcode> ::= SPC    -- speak continue
                 | SPW    -- speak wait
                 | MMWA   -- motor move wait absolute
                 | MMCA   -- motor move continue absolute
.)l
The indexed Robot Control Language instructions have an instruction
format which is the same as the 6800 JMP and JSR instructions;
the operand may be either an absolute address (extended mode), or
an indexed address (indicated by a trailing X on the address field).
In either case, the operand indicates the address of a speech string
or of a motor control command word.
.(l I
.ne 4
<rcl time> ::= <rcl t opcode> [ , ] <expression>
<rcl t opcode> ::= SLEEP  -- sleep (units of 10 seconds)
                 | PAUSE  -- pause (units of 1/16 second)
.)l
The argument to the time instructions is an expression which
specifies the time interval involved; this must evaluate to a 16 bit
unsigned integer.
.(l I
.ne 2
<rcl jump> ::= <rcl j opcode> [ , ] <expression>
<rcl j opcode> ::= JSP    -- jump if speaking
.)l
The Robot Control Language jump if speaking differ from the
6800 JMP instruction in that indexed addresses are not allowed;
the operand must be a simple expression which is interpreted as
the extended address of a string of phonemes, which will be appended
to the current speech string.
.(l I
.ne 6
<rcl motormove> ::= <rcl m opcode> [ , ] <rcl m operand>
<rcl m opcode> ::= MMWAI  -- motor move wait absolute immediate
                 | MMCAI  -- motor move continue absolute immediate
                 | MMWRI  -- motor move wait relative immediate
                 | MMCRI  -- motor move continue relative immediate
.ne 6
<rcl m operand> ::= <motor> , <speed> , <dir> , <dist>
<motor> ::= <expression>  -- between 0 and 7
<speed> ::= <expression>  -- between 1 and 3
<dir> ::= <expression>    -- either 0 or 1
<dist> ::= <expression>   -- between 0 and 1023
.)l
The immediate motor move commands take 4 operands which are compressed
into a 16 bit operand in the assembled version; note that, although the
distance field may be between 0 and 1023, the full range is only meaningful
for the drive motor.  For other motors, see the comments below.
.(l I
.ne 10
<rcl moveall> ::= MMALL [ , ] <extend> , <shoulder> , <rotate>
                  , <pivot> , <gripper> , <head> , <steering>
<extend> ::= <expression>    -- arm extension     (00/in to 152/out)
<shoulder> ::= <expression>  -- shoulder rotation (00/down to 134/up)
<rotate> ::= <expression>    -- wrist rotation    (00/ccw to 147/cw)
<pivot> ::= <expression>     -- wrist pivot       (00/up to 165/down)
<gripper> ::= <expression>   -- gripper opening   (00/closed to 117/open)
<head> ::= <expression>      -- head rotation     (00/left to 194/right)
<steering> ::= <expression>  -- steering rotation (00/left to 147/right)
.)l
Each of the operands of the motor move all command must evaluate
to a single byte value, which is interpreted as being unsigned, and
must be in the range indicated in the comments above.
.pp
The memory addressed motor move commands, MMWA and MMCA, refer with their
operand to a motor control command word elsehwere in memory.  In order
to ease the construction of such words at assembly time, the following
directive is supported:
.(l I
.ne 2
<symbolic directive> ::= MCCW [ , ] <rcl m operand>
.)l
The operand to the motor control command word directive has the same
format as the operands on the immediate motor move instructions.
.pp
To further facilitate the assembly of motor motion commands, the
following definitions should be included at the start of each program
which uses the Robot Control Language instructions:
.(l I
.ne 10
; motor names
DRIVE    = 0
EXTEND   = 1
SHOULDER = 2
ROTATE   = 3
PIVOT    = 4
GRIPPER  = 5
HEAD     = 6
STEERING = 7
.ne 5
; motor speeds
SLOW     = 1
MEDIUM   = 2
FAST     = 3
.ne 6
; motor directions
FORWARD  = 0  ;drive direction
BACKWARD = 1
IN       = 0  ;arm extension
OUT      = 1
ARMDOWN  = 0  ;shoulder motion
ARMUP    = 1
.ne 5
WRISTCCW = 0  ;wrist rotation
WRISTCW  = 1
WRISTUP  = 0  ;wrist pivot
WRISTDOWN= 1
.ne 5
CLOSE    = 0  ;gripper opening
OPEN     = 1
LEFT     = 0  ;head and steering rotation
RIGHT    = 1
.)l
This allows motor motions to be specified by commands
such as the following:
.(l I
.ne 6
MMWRI DRIVE,SLOW,FORWARD,20
MMCRI DRIVE,SLOW,FORWARD,40
MMWRI STEERING,FAST,LEFT,32
MMWRI STEERING,FAST,RIGHT,32
MMWRI DRIVE,SLOW,FORWARD,20
.)l
.bp
.sh 1 "THE SMAL LINKER"
.pp
When the SMAL Hero assembler processes a source program,
it produces an object file.
Object files are encoded in the SMAL Hero object sublanguage, and may be
linked into a load file by the SMAL Hero assembler.
When the assembler is used for this linkage editing function,
it is controlled by a linker control program, encoded in the
SMAL Hero linker control sublanguage.
As a linker, the assembler may process object library files in addition
to simple object files; such object library files must be encoded in the
SMAL Hero object library sublanguage.
Finally, the output of the assembler when it is used as a linkage
editor is either another object file or a load file encoded in the
SMAL Hero loader sublanguage.
.pp
As the terminology suggests, object files, linker control files, and
load files are all legal SMAL Hero
programs, encoded in particular subsets of the
language.  Object files have less symbolic information in them than
source files, and load files have almost no symbolic content
(what remains is concerned only with establishing relocatability).
SMAL Hero linker control files and object library files consist primarily of
information concerning how to find each part of the program to be linked
and when to link it.
.sh 2 "The Linker Control Sublanguage"
.pp
A SMAL Hero linker control program consists of an optional sequence of
linker controls, followed by a sequence of object specifications
which name the files to be linked, followed by an optional common
specification.
.(l I
.ne 4
<linker control program> ::= { <linker control> }
                             { <object specification> }
                             [ <common specification> ]
                               <end of file>
.)l
If the object files to be linked do not reference any common regions,
and if the result to be produced is intended for loading only, then
the linker control program need not contain anything but object
specifications.
.(l I
<object specification> ::= USE <quoted string> <line end>
.)l
Each object specification is a USE directive which names either an
object file or a library file.  The object files named will be loaded
sequentially in memory, while the library files will be searched to see
if they contain definitions of any symbols referenced by the programs
already loaded.  If so, appropriate material from the library files
is also loaded.
If the files "MAIN", "SUB1", and "SUB2" are SMAL object files, the
following linker control program could be used to link them:
.(l I
.ne 3
USE "MAIN"
USE "SUB1"
USE "SUB2"
.)l
If there is some external symbol used by one of these programs but not
defined in another of them, an "undefined symbol" error will be produced
on the terminal and in the listing file.
.pp
If any of the object files to be linked contain references to commons,
the linker must be told where to place the commons.  The common
specification may take a number of forms.  In the simplest form, the
linker is told to place the commons in memory immediately after
everything else:
.(l I
<common specification> ::= C = . <line end>
.)l
The following example loader control program illustrates this form of use
of commons:
.(l I
.ne 5
USE "MAIN"
USE "SUB1"
USE "SUB2"
USE "LIBRARY"
C=.
.)l
Note that the common specification is placed after all object specifications,
including any which reference libraries.
.sh 2 "Linker Control Functions"
.pp
If programs are to be loaded in random access memory in a conventional
way, the above should suffice.  On the other hand, there are times when common
regions should not be loaded in the same memory as normal object code,
for example, on systems where one is to be in read-only memory and the
other in read-write memory.  In this case, the following form can be
used:
.(l I
<linker control> ::= C = <number> <line end>
.)l
Here, the absolute address where the first common is to be placed is
stated, and other commons will be placed successively in memory after it.
Note that this form of common specification is used before the sequence of
object specifications, not after.
.pp
The normal action of the SMAL assembler, when used as a linker, is to
produce a relocatable load file.  This normal behavior may be changed in
a number of ways by the linker controls.  For example, to create an
absolute load file, the following control can be used:
.(l I
<linker control> ::= . = <number> <line end>
.)l
For an example of the use of this form, consider a system with 4K bytes
of read-write memory starting at address $0000 and with 16K bytes of
read-only memory starting at address $1000.  If the source programs for
the object files involved were consistent in their use of common for all
read-write variables, maintaining a strict separation between
code and data, then the following linker control file could be used
to prepare a load file to be used in "burning" the read-only memory:
.(l I
.ne 5
\&.=$1000
C=$0000
USE "MAIN"
USE "SUB1"
USE "SUB2"
.)l
.ne 2
Note the use of the common specification to control the placement of all
commons in the read-write region.
.pp
At times, there may be some commons or other external symbols which must
be placed in specific memory locations, for example, a program may reference
a block of perepheral device control registers as a common, allowing
adjustments for the particular machine configuration to be made at linkage
time.  In this case, the following form can be used:
.(l I
<linker control> ::= R<identifier> = <number> <line end>
.)l
Note that an external name is a legal identifier which begins with the
letter R.  External names are formed by adding the R prefix to the identifier
used internally in a source program as operands on an EXT, INT, or COMMON
directives.  External names should only be defined by a linker control
directive when they are not defined by an INT directive in one of the object
files to be linked.
.pp
When it is necessary to specify the size of a common at linkage time
instead of at assembly time, the following linker control may be used:
.(l I
<linker control> ::= S<identifier> = <number> <line end>
.)l
When this form is used, it will override the size of the given common
declared in the programs being linked.
Thus, it is even possible to decrease the size of a common below that
which a program declared that it needed.  When this is intended,
the common itself should probably begin with an external reference
which is expected to be set by the linker control program to indicate
the size of the common actually allocated.
.pp
Finally, if the starting address of a program is not specified internally
in any of the routines being linked, it may be specified at linkage time
as follows:
.(l I
<linker control> ::= S R<identifier>
.)l
Where the identifier is an internal symbol in one of the files being
linked.  An error will result if this directive is used and one of the
linked routines already has a specified starting address.
.sh 2 "Producing an Object File as Linker Output"
.pp
It is possible to direct the SMAL Hero assembler, as a linker, to
produce an object file as output instead of a load file.  This is done,
for example, when the set of object files being linked does not comprise
an entire program, but only part of one; thus, the result may still contain
unresolved references to external symbols, and it may define internal symbols.
In this case, linker control directives must be used to inform the
assembler, as a linker, which external symbols are to be exported by or
imported to the resulting object file.  Export is done as follows:
.(l I
.ne 2
<linker control> ::= <identifier> = R<identifier> <line end>
                     INT <identifier> <line end>
.)l
The identifiers used on the two lines of this directive should be the same.
Import is done as follows:
.(l I
.ne 2
<linker control> ::= EXT <identifier> <line end>
                     R<identifier> = <identifier> <line end>
.)l
Again, the identifiers must be the same, and the effect is to
make all references to the given external name from within any of the
object files being linked into references to a symbol
external to the object file resulting from the linkage process.
The following example SMAL linker control file illustrates these forms:
.(l I
.ne 7
EXT DATA
RDATA = DATA
ENTRY = RENTRY
INT ENTRY
USE "ENTRY"
USE "TABLE"
C=.
.)l
If the object file "ENTRY" was generated from source containing "EXT\ TABLE"
and "INT\ ENTRY", and the object file "TABLE" was generated from source
containing "INT\ TABLE" and "EXT\ DATA" then the result of processing
this linkage control file will be a relocatable object file referencing the
external
name "RDATA" and defining the external name "RENTRY".
.pp
When a common area declared in one of the programs being linked is to be
preserved as a common area in the resulting object file, thus allowing it to
be shared with other programs linked to the resulting object file, a special
form of import/export must be used:
.(l I
.ne 2
<linker control> ::= COMMON <identifier> , S<identifier> <line end>
                     R<identifier> = <identifier> <line end>
.)l
Note that the identifiers must all be the same, and that the
special identifier consisting of an S prefix attached to the common name
gives the declared size of the first instance of that common.
.pp
Any commons used by the programs being linked
which are not to be preserved in the final object file will usually be
lumped onto the end of that file as local data by using a "C=." common
specification at the end of the linker control file.  An alternative is
to lump the commons which are not individually preserved into
a single common in the resulting object file.
This is useful, for example, when the program must be able to be stored in
read-only memory and the commons must be stored in read-write memory.
When this is desired, the following form can be used:
.(l I
.ne 2
<linker control> ::= COMMON <identifier> , C <line end>
                     C = <identifier> <line end>
.)l
Note, here, that all of the identifiers must be the same.
The following modified version of the previous example uses this form
to allow the result to be linked with commons separated from normal
relocatable code:
.(l I
.ne 8
EXT DATA
RDATA = DATA
ENTRY = RENTRY
INT ENTRY
COMMON ENTRYCOM,C
C=ENTRYCOM
USE "ENTRY"
USE "TABLE"
.)l
Note the use in the above example of the convention that the common used
to store data for a particular routine (or, as in this case, collection
of routines) has its name formed by appending the suffix "COM" to the name of
the entry point for that routine.
.pp
It should be noted that there is an important restriction on the set of
symbols which can be exported from or imported to an object file created as
linker output:  The symbols 'R' and 'CT' have special meaning during the
linkage process; the linker also reserves symbols formed with 'R' as a prefix
on an identifier being exported by one of the files being linked, or with 'R'
or 'S' as a prefix on the name of a common.
.sh 2 "Linker Control Macros"
.pp
The last four linker control directives discussed above are complex
enough that it is conventional to package them as macros.  The definitions
of these macros should be inserted with a USE directive at the head of the
linker control file.  Typically, the macros might be packaged in a file
called "LINKMACS", so the following new linker directive would be
introduced:
.(l I
<linker control> ::= USE "LINKMACS"
.)l
Inclusion of this linker control directive at the head of a file would
define the following new linker control directives:
.(l I
.ne 4
<linker control> ::= IMPORTS <identifier>
                   | EXPORTS <identifier>
                   | PRESERVECOMMON <identifier>
                   | LUMPCOMMONS <identifier>
.)l
These would be defined by the following macros:
.(l I
.ne 4
MACRO IMPORTS NAME
  EXT NAME
  R'NAME = NAME
ENDMAC

.ne 4
MACRO EXPORTS NAME
  NAME = R'NAME
  INT NAME
ENDMAC

.ne 4
MACRO PRESERVECOMMON NAME
  COMMON NAME,S'NAME
  R'NAME = NAME
ENDMAC

.ne 4
MACRO LUMPCOMMONS NAME
  COMMON NAME,C
  C=NAME
ENDMAC
.)l
In terms of these macros, the last example used above could be
recoded as follows:
.(l I
.ne 6
USE "LINKMACS"
IMPORTS DATA
EXPORTS ENTRY
LUMPCOMMONS ENTRYCOM
USE "ENTRY"
USE "TABLE"
.)l
.bp
.sh 1 "SMAL HERO OBJECT LIBRARIES"
.pp
A SMAL Hero object library file is a file of references to object code files.
Library files are textual in form, and may be maintained by a conventional
text editor.  Unlike the approach taken on many other systems, library files
do not contain object code, but only contain references to object files.
The text of the library file itself is written in the SMAL Hero object library
sublanguage.
.(l I
<library file> ::= { <object reference> } <end of file>
.)l
Each object reference in a library file specifies the name of an object
file and the names of one or more symbols which that object file
defines:
.(l I
.ne 5
<object reference> ::= IF <symbol list> <line end>
                         USE <quoted string> <line end>
                       ENDIF <line end>
<symbol list> ::= <symbol reference> { ! <symbol reference> }
<symbol reference> ::= FWD ( R<identifier> )
.)l
The effect of an object reference is to cause the assembler, when
used as a linker, to link the indicated object file if the previously
loaded material has made any references to the symbols in the symbol list
and the referenced symbols have not been previously defined.
Each symbol in the symbol list takes the form of an external name, that is,
an identifier consisting of an "R" prefix on an identifier used as an
operand of a COMMON or INT directive in the object file.
The following is a legal SMAL Hero library file:
.(l I
.ne 3
IF FWD(RSIN) ! FWD(RCOS) ! FWD(RTAN)
  USE "TRIGFUNC"
ENDIF
.ne 3
IF FWD(REXP) ! FWD(RPOWER)
  USE "EXPFUNC"
ENDIF
.ne 3
IF FWD(RLOG) ! FWD(RLN)
  USE "LOGFUNC"
ENDIF
.ne 3
IF FWD(RFLOAT)
  USE "FLOATFUNC"
ENDIF
.ne 3
IF FWD(RFIX)
  USE "FIXFUNC"
ENDIF
.)l
Note that, if one of the object files in the library references another,
that file should be placed before the file it relies on.
It is legal to repeat an object reference any number of times in an object
library file, and it is legal to have multiple object references with different
symbol references which refer to the same object file.
.pp
When SMAL Hero is run under a hierarchically sturctured file system,
each library should be organized as a single directory, with the library
file itself stored under that directory in a file named "DIR", and all
other object files in the library stored in the same directory.
If the USE directive is implemented as suggested in the section on
miscellaneous assembly directives, this will allow
relative file names to be used in the library, so that the library
as a whole can be copied using a tree copy operation without
the need to modify any internal file names.
.sh 2 "Library Macros"
.pp
The form of an object reference is complex enough that it is convenient
to package it as a macro, thus introducing the following new form:
.(l I
<object reference> ::= LIB <quoted string> , <identifier> <line end>
.)l
The macro to do this should be included in the file "LINKMACS" used for
linkage editor macros; this macro can be defined as follows:
.(l I
.ne 5
MACRO LIB FILE,NAME
  IF FWD(R'NAME)
    USE FILE
  ENDIF
ENDMAC
.)l
Using this form, the above example library could be recoded as follows:
.(l I
.ne 9
LIB "TRIGFUNC",SIN
LIB "TRIGFUNC",COS
LIB "TRIGFUNC",TAN
LIB "EXPFUNC",EXP
LIB "EXPFUNC",POWER
LIB "LOGFUNC",LOG
LIB "LOGFUNC",LN
LIB "FLOATFUNC",FLOAT
LIB "FIXFUNC",FIX
.)l
Note that this sequence of macro calls does not expand to the same
sequence of directives as was used in the previous example,
but that it expands to a sequence of directives with the same effect.
.bp
.sh 1 "THE SMAL HERO LOADER AND OBJECT LANGUAGES"
.pp
SMAL Hero load files contain text in the loader sublanguage.
The loader sublanguage is a subset of the SMAL Hero object sublanguage,
which is, in turn, a subset of the entire language.
These sublanguages are very small subsets of the entire SMAL Hero language;
they only contain the bare minimum of information needed to satisfy
their purpose.
.sh 2 "The Loader Sublanguage"
.pp
A SMAL Hero load file consists of a fixed header followed by a sequence of
load directives:
.(l I
.ne 3
<load file> ::= R=.<end of line>
              { <load directive><end of line> }
                <end of file>
.)l
No blanks or blank lines are used within a load file unless they are
specifically required by some particular load directive.
.pp
There are 4 basic load directives which control the setting of the loader
location counter, the loading of bytes, the loading of words,
and the setting of the starting address.
.(l I
.ne 4
<load directive> ::= .=<load value>
                   | B<load value>
                   | W<load value>
                   | S<load value>
.)l
The value on each load directive may be either an absolute (hexadecimal)
integer, or a relocatable value, specified by the use of the
symbol "R".
.(l I
.ne 4
<load value> ::= <hexadecimal number>
               | <hexadecimal number>+R
               | <space>R
<hexadecimal number> ::= $<extended number>
.)l
Every load file always ends with the location counter set just beyond the
highest relocatable location used by that load file.
.sh 2 "A SMAL Loader"
.pp
The following Pascal function will read and load one SMAL load file from
the specified file and place it in the global array called "memory", relocated
relative to the given relocation base.
A global variable "start" is set to the starting address of the loaded program.
The function returns the address of the first location beyond the last loaded
relocatable location:
.(l I
.ne 9
function load (f:textfile; loc:integer): integer;
var temp: integer;
    ch: char;
    base: integer { the relocation base };

    procedure value;
    begin
         read(f,ch);
.ne 6
         if ch = '$' then begin
              temp := 0;
              repeat
                   read(f,ch);
                   if ch in ['0'..'9'] then begin
{precision}             temp := (16 * temp) + (ord(ch) - ord('0'));
.ne 9
                   end else if ch in ['A'..'F'] then begin
{precision}             temp := (16 * temp) + (ord(ch) - ord('A')) + 10;
                   end;
              until (ch = '+') or eoln(f);
              {assert ch in ['+','A'..'F','0'..'9']}
              if ch = '+' then begin
                   read(f,ch) {assert ch='R'};
                   temp := temp + base;
              end;
.ne 7
         end else begin
              {assert ch=' '}
              read(f,ch) {assert ch='R'};
              temp := base;
         end;
         {assert eoln(f)}
    end {value};

.ne 10
begin
     reset(f);
     while not(eof(f)) do begin
          read(f,ch);
          case ch of
               ".": begin
                         read(f,ch) {assert ch='='};
                         value;
                         loc := temp;
                    end;
.ne 5
               "B": begin
                         value;
                         memory[loc] := temp;
                         loc := loc + 1;
                    end;
.ne 6
               "W": begin
                         value;
{precision}              memory[loc] := temp div 256;
{precision}              memory[loc] + 1 := temp mod 256;
                         loc := loc + 2;
                    end;
.ne 4
               "S": begin
                         value;
                         start := temp;
                    end;
.ne 11
               "R": begin
                         read(f,ch) {assert ch='='};
                         read(f,ch) {assert ch='.'};
                         base := loc;
                    end
          end {case};
          {assert eoln(f)}
          readln(f);
     end {while};
     load := loc;
end {load};
.)l
The above code is not resiliant in the sense that it makes no effort to
detect errors or make use of the redundancy inherent in the object language;
if such resiliancy is desired, comments included in it give assertions
about conditions which can be tested.
.pp
This code assumes that the arithmetic operations available to it support
unsigned integer values between 0 and 65535.  If it is to be used on
a machine supporting only signed integers in the range -32768 to 32767, the
the multiplication, mod, and div operators on the lines marked
with a "precision" comment must be modified.
If the code is rewritten in machine language, the multiply and divide
operators should be replaced with shift operators, and the modulus operator
should be replaced with a masking operator.
.sh 2 "The Object Sublanguage"
.pp
A SMAL Hero object file consists of a fixed header identical to that used
on load files, followed by a sequence of object directives, followed
by an optional sequence of internal definitions:
.(l I
.ne 4
<object file> ::= R=.<end of line>
                { <object directive><end of line> }
                { <internal definition><end of line> }
                  <end of file>
.)l
As in load files, spaces and blank lines are not included in object files
unless they are explicitly needed.
In the case of object files, however, this rule need not be strictly followed;
spaces are allowed wherever the assembler will accept them.
.pp
There is a form of object directive which corresponds to each of the
loader directives, but the forms of the allowed values are more complex,
and a new compound directive is used to manage common declarations:
.(l I
.ne 5
<object directive> ::= .=<object value>
                     | B<object value>
                     | W<object value>
                     | S<object value>
                     | <common definition>
.)l
The value specified on each basic object directive may be either an absolute
integer or a relocatable value and a specification of the relocation base:
.(l I
.ne 4
<object value> ::= <hexadecimal number>
                 | <hexadecimal number>+<relocation base>
                 | <space><relocation base>
<relocation base> ::= R | R<identifier>
.)l
Note that "R" is the default relocation base, and that any other form
of relocation base is a reference to an external symbol defined in the internal
definition section of some other object file.
.pp
Common declarations in a SMAL Hero
source program result in the following special
form of object directive:
.(l I
.ne 10
<common definition> ::= IF\\DEF(S<identifier>)<end of line>
                         S<identifier>=$<extended number><end of line>
                        ENDIF<end of line>
                        IF\\DEF(R<identifier>)<end of line>
                         R<identifier>=C<end of line>
                         C=C+S<identifier><end of line>
                         CT=.<end of line>
                         .=C<end of line>
                         .=CT<end of line>
                        ENDIF
.)l
Each common directive in a SMAL Hero source program will result in a sequence
of object code patterned on the above, where all
identifiers are replaced by the common name, and the extended number is
replaced by the common size in hexadecimal.
.pp
If the source program contains any internal symbol definitions, these
will be gathered together at the end of the object file and encoded
in the following form:
.(l I
<internal definition> ::= R<identifier>=<object value>
.)l
.bp
.sh 1 "SMAL HERO INSTALLATION AND MAINTENANCE"
.pp
The SMAL Hero assembler is written in Pascal with the intent that it be easily
transportable.
The following sections introduce the problems which will be encountered in
installing it on a new machine, and in removing machine
independence in order to
improve efficiency.
.sh 2 "Installation"
.pp
The interface between the assembler and the file system of the host
machine will probably have to be changed when the assembler is moved to
a new machine.  In order for the USE directive to be properly executed,
the assembler must run in an environment where it has direct access to
the file system; use in versions of Pascal where program parameters
are the only way of connecting a Pascal file to an actual file will thus be
somewhat inconvenient.
.pp
The global procedure "getfiles" is used to encapsulate the problem of getting
the name of the source file from the user and using this to synthesize the
names of the object and listing files.  These textual file names are stored
in the global variables "infile[1]", "outfile", and "objfile", which are used,
respectively, to open the text files "inp1", "output", and "obj".
The text files "inp2" and "inp3" are opened by the procedure "insert", which
is called whenever a USE directive is encountered.  The text files "inp1",
"inp2", and "inp3" can thus be considered to be a short stack of files used
for input.  If more levels of nested USE directives are to be allowed, this
stack must be enlarged.
Note that the operation of binding a Pascal text file to a file with
a particular textual name is accomplished by the "reset" and "rewrite"
procedures in some versions of Pascal and by a special "open" procedure
in others.  In the latter cases, calls to "open" will have to be added before
each call to "reset" or "rewrite".
.pp
All matters concerning listing generation are encapsulated within the
procedures "listline" and "newpage"; these will require changes if the
system being used does not respond to an ASCII FF character to initiate
a formfeed.  Typical changes might include the introduction of FORTRAN
style carriage control characters at the start of each listing line,
or the printing of sequences of blank lines instead of special page
feed characters.
.pp
In addition to converting the SMAL Hero assembler to use the appropriate file
system, the sizes of the various static data structures must be specified.
These are all set with constant declarations in the main program, but the
values of the constants in the machine independent version are all set
low enough that the static data structures are easily filled for testing
purposes.  The constants "symsize", "opcodes", and "linelen"
may be set as large as desired with no change to other parts of the
assembler.
The constant "poolsize" is limited to 32767 by the code for "pushint" and
"popint", which are used to push and pop pointers into the string pool on
the stack within the pool.
A number of other constants can be modified to do such things as change the
listing format or change the number of allowed macro parameters.  Limits on the
values of these constants are documented in comments on their declarations.
.pp
The global array "ascii" is used to associate internal Pascal characters
with their ASCII representations.  On versions of Pascal supporting more than
64 printing characters in their character set, the "initascii" procedure
should be extended to initialize the additional characters.  On some ASCII
implementations of Pascal, it may be possible to eliminate the
"ascii" array and use the "ord" function (but beware of the default
setting of the high bit; in SMAL Hero, this shoud be consistently set to zero!)
In some environments, it may be reasonable to add an EBCDIC directive
and a new supporting translation array in order to allow use of the
EBCDIC character set.  When this is done, the ASCII directive should
remain, and quoted strings should retain their ASCII interpretation as values
unless some special new quote mark, such as `, is used to indicate an EBCDIC
interpretation.
.sh 2 "Efficiency"
.pp
The machine independent version of the SMAL Hero assembler was written to be
easily moved to a new machine, but this does not imply that it will run
efficiently in that environment.  In fact, transportability and efficiency
are in direct conflict, especially in the areas of bit packing and
integer arithmetic.  If efficiency becomes an issue, the following steps
outline the order in which different changes to the assembler should be made
to remove machine independance and speed things up:
.pp
Many Pascal implementations generate a considerable amount of code to check
such things as array bounds and subrange variables.  Turning off this checking
is frequently easy, and has resulted in improvements of about 15% for the
SMAL assembler under the Hull R-mode compiler for the Prime 750 computer.
The assembler is coded to be resiliant enough that turning off the
run-time checking should not threaten its reliability, but
if any changes are made, the checks should be turned back on
until all is secure.
.pp
The routine "writehex" used for object code and listing generation uses
some cumbersome code to guarantee consistent results on machines with different
integer representations.  Since this routine is called three to four times
for each line of code assembled, it may pay to recode it in machine code
using shift and mask instructions to extract and convert hexadecimal digits.
Things will be even better in versions of Pascal which directly support
hexadecimal output.
.pp
The lexical analysis routine for parsing numeric constants has a significant
amount of cumbersome code to allow it to handle unsigned 16 bit values on a
machine using a signed 16 bit representation.  If the SMAL assembler is run
on a machine with a larger word size, this code can be significantly
simplified.
.pp
A key cause of inefficiency in the SMAL Hero assembler is the implementation
of 16 bit two's complement arithmetic in a manner which will run consistently
on any machine which supports the desired range of integer values.
The routines responsible for this are grouped at head of the assembler
and may easily be changed to use external routines coded in machine code.
On versions of Pascal implemented with 16 bit arithmetic,
it may be sufficient to simply turn off run-time checking and use the built
in operations; thus, a call to "add(a,b)" may be equivalent to "a+b" when
run-time checking is disabled.  Although this change will have a significant
effect on the speed of these operations, they are infrequently used during the
assembly process, and probably do not contribute significantly to the
time taken to assemble typical programs.
